  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA (descriptive, verb-disciplined) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>The United States Defense Department announced on September 11, 2026 that CENTCOM had conducted strikes on air defense installations and coastal surveillance facilities in Hormuzgan Province, southern Iran. The stated justification was the interdiction of ongoing IRGC mine-deployment operations in the Strait of Hormuz — the same operations that had held commercial traffic to a 10-day rolling average of 14 vessels per day against a pre-war baseline of approximately 85. Trump stated on Truth Social that the strikes targeted the 'Iranians' failed attempt at adding sea mines.' CENTCOM released post-strike imagery of two destroyed radar sites. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span>, <span class="cite">F6</span></p>

      <p>The IRGC responded within hours with a coordinated ballistic missile salvo directed at three separate US military positions: the drone hangar at Prince Hassan Airbase in Jordan, a US logistics facility in Bahrain, and Camp Titin near the Gulf of Aqaba. CENTCOM confirmed incoming fire at all three locations and acknowledged structural damage at the Jordan facility; the IRGC claimed destruction of RQ-4 and MQ-9 drone infrastructure and casualties among technical personnel. This constituted the ninth consecutive day of documented bilateral offensive action between CENTCOM and the IRGC. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>Strait of Hormuz commercial shipping data tracked by Straits.live through September 9-10 recorded a 10-day rolling average of 14 civilian vessels per day, with a single-day count of 7 on September 9. No liquefied natural gas tankers cleared the strait on September 9; a previous single-day low of 5 vessels occurred on September 8. War-risk insurance premiums for Hormuz passage remained at prohibitive levels for commercial operators, with no named insurer announcing a reinstatement of cover through September 12. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>

      <p>Iran's Foreign Ministry issued no statement on ceasefire negotiations or mediated negotiation sessions on September 11 or 12. FM Araghchi's most recent public comment, dated September 8, characterized Iran's position as conditional on 'genuine US de-escalation' — a formulation that falls below the held-session threshold under F3's ambiguity rule and does not constitute qualifying readiness for talks. No Oman, Qatar, or Pakistan diplomatic readout announced a convened or scheduled session in the September 3-12 window. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>

      <p>Israeli pre-election polling published around September 6 showed a continuing near-tie between Likud (Benjamin Netanyahu) and Yashar (Gadi Eisenkot): an i24NEWS/Direct Polls survey put Likud at 27 mandates versus Yashar at 22, while a concurrent KAN News poll reversed the order, with Yashar at 24 mandates and Likud at 21. Neither bloc reached the 61-seat governing threshold in any survey. Netanyahu confirmed he will stand as Likud's candidate. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

