  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN (descriptive, ~200 words) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>IRNA and Tasnim led September 11-12 coverage with the IRGC ballistic missile response to what they described as 'repeated American terrorist aggression against the Iranian nation.' State media named the Prince Hassan Airbase drone hangar as a specifically targeted facility and cited the IRGC's claim of destroying RQ-4 and MQ-9 platforms along with casualty claims among technical personnel. The framing presented the retaliation as 'proportionate and legally mandated defense' with no editorial language conceding offensive escalation. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>IRGC Spokesperson Brigadier General Ali Mohammad Naini, in an IRIB interview broadcast September 12, stated that Iran had 'activated enhanced deterrence postures' following the US Hormuzgan strikes and named the Prince Hassan drone hangar as a specifically targeted facility. No named IRGC or government official announced a halt in operations or any readiness for talks. Naini's statement held open the option of further action without announcing a specific follow-on timeline — consistent with prior post-strike IRGC communications. → supports <span class="cite">F6</span> without move</p>

      <p>Kayhan's editorial board published a column on September 12 calling for 'full and permanent closure of Hormuz until the last American bomb ceases.' Pezeshkian's government has not adopted this as official policy; no operational order enacting full closure has been issued. The column documents the domestic pressure on the executive from the hardline camp but carries no operational force, and Kayhan has published comparable editorials at least six times since June 2026 without the IRGC operationalizing full closure. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

